#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""xui-tunnel - one outbound, several tunnels to the same server abroad.

    xui-tunnel discover [--json]            tunnels on this hub, where each lands, which paths ride it
    xui-tunnel list [--json]                paths, the tunnel addresses each may use, which one is live
    xui-tunnel set NAME ADDR[,ADDR...] [--auto on|off]
                                            the addresses NAME may use, best first
    xui-tunnel use NAME ADDR [--json]       move NAME (and NAME-mux) onto ADDR now
    xui-tunnel add NAME ADDR [--json]       put one more address on NAME's list (by hand; tested, kept either way)
    xui-tunnel scan NAME [--save] [--json]  try NAME on every tunnel's far address; --save lists the ones that answer
    xui-tunnel test NAME ADDR [--json]      would NAME work on ADDR? (a throwaway Xray, not the live one)
    xui-tunnel auto                         what the timer runs: leave a dead tunnel, return to the best,
                                            and rescan every path once an hour
    xui-tunnel mix NAME [--json]            how NAME's traffic is shared between its tunnels
    xui-tunnel mix NAME on ADDR,ADDR[,...] [--how smart|random|roundrobin] [--weights 2,1]
                                            use those tunnels at the same time (first = fallback)
    xui-tunnel mix NAME off                 back to one tunnel at a time (the first of the mix)
    xui-tunnel mix NAME pin ADDR|none       only ADDR for now, without a restart (until x-ui restarts)
    xui-tunnel order NAME auto|manual       auto: the timer ranks NAME's tunnels itself (upload speed,
                                            packet loss, stability); manual: the list order, as set
    xui-tunnel upload NAME [ADDR] [--json]  measure upload speed through NAME's tunnels now
    xui-tunnel steer [on|off] [--json]      users moved off the busy tunnel for a heavy upload

A GRE tunnel and an AWG tunnel to the same server reach the same Xray listener, only at a
different address (10.0.1.2 vs 10.0.10.1). So instead of one path per tunnel, a path keeps
a list of addresses and this moves its outbound between them. The switch goes through Xray's
API (rmo + ado): no x-ui restart, only the connections on the dead tunnel drop. The panel's
template is updated too, so the next restart keeps it.

Routed tunnels (GRE, IPIP, WireGuard, AmneziaWG) are used by their far address. A sing-box tun
is used differently: the address stays the same and the outbound is sent through the tun
(Xray's sendThrough = the tun's own address; the hub's policy routing sends that into the tun,
sing-box carries it to its server, and the connection lands on the same listener there).
Such an entry is written ADDR@IFACE, for example 10.0.1.2@sb0.
Any other tun (gost, Backhaul, ...) is a routed tunnel with a peer: its far server is found from
the process holding the tun open - its one remote connection, or, when it has several, the one
whose byte counters move during a short ping burst through the tunnel (cached per process for an
hour). A Geneve/VXLAN/GRE tunnel whose remote sits inside another tunnel is that tunnel's server.
A scan does not trust the guess of where a tunnel lands: it starts NAME's own outbound on each
tunnel's far address and keeps the ones that fetch a page, so it finds tunnels whose far end is
unknown too. xui-path runs a scan right after it adds a path.
Settings live under "tunnels" in /etc/tunnel-guard.json, state in /var/lib/tunnel-guard/tunnels.json.

Mixing: instead of one tunnel at a time, NAME's new connections are shared between two or more
of its tunnels (an Xray balancer over one copy of NAME per tunnel; see lib/xuimix.py). "smart"
keeps every healthy tunnel in the mix and drops one that loses pings or turns slow, "random"
and "roundrobin" share by weight. Xray's observatory skips a dead tunnel by itself; the timer
only tests each tunnel every few minutes for the page and tells Telegram when one drops out or
comes back. Turning it on or off restarts x-ui once (balancers and the observatory are not hot).

Packet loss: every minute each tunnel of a mix is pinged (25 pings, 0.1 s apart, all tunnels
at once) and the last 6 minutes are kept. A tunnel losing 3% or more, and at least 2 points
more than the cleanest one, while a clean tunnel is left, is taken out of the mix in the
running Xray (its copies are removed through the API; no restart, the panel's template is
untouched) and Telegram is told. It comes back after 3 clean minutes. When every tunnel loses
alike nothing is taken out: the trouble is past the tunnels. After an x-ui restart the live
copies are compared with what should run and the exclusion is put back.

Ranking: every 30 minutes each tunnel of a path uploads 6 MB to a speed test (through a
throwaway Xray, so no user is billed for it), every minute each one is pinged (25 pings), and each
check is remembered as answered or not. A tunnel's score is its upload speed (median of the last
three), cut by its packet loss (20% loss = nothing left) and by its instability (answered share,
squared). With the order on auto the path moves to the best tunnel when it scores a quarter better
than the current one three minutes running, at most every 30 minutes; a dead tunnel is left for
the best one that answers. Moving the path by hand, or reordering its list, turns the order to
manual (the list as given) until it is set to auto again.

Heavy upload: every minute the users' upload counters are read from Xray (read only: the panel's
billing counts on them and they are never reset). A user uploading 4 Mbit/s or more (heavy_up_mbps)
for two minutes running gets their own rules, ahead of the path's rules, to a copy of the path on
its best other tunnel - new connections of that user only, so a big upload does not fill the
tunnel everyone shares and gets a free one. Ten quiet minutes later the rules go. Changed live
(xuilive, no restart), never while the guard has failed the primary path over.
"""
